(def thread-page "(->> value form ...)\n\nThreads a value through each form as its last argument.\n\n`(->> 3 (- 10) (* 2))` is `(* 2 (- 10 3))`. A bare name in place of a form\nis called with the value alone.\n\n(->> 3 (- 10) (* 2))\n=> 14\n\n(->> (range 0 3) collect)\n=> (vec 0 1 2)")
(def sum-page "(defn sum (values))\n\nAdds the items of an iterator from left to right.\n\nAn empty iterator gives 0. An overflow panics in a debug build, as `+`\ndoes. A vector passes and yields copies of its numbers. `(iter items)` is an\nerror, as its items are borrows, and so is a map.\n\n(sum (vec 1 2 3))\n=> 6\n\n(-> (range 0 0) sum)\n=> 0")
(def assoc-page "(defmacro assoc (value key more ...))\n\nReturns a struct or a collection with new values at its keys.\n\nA keyword key sets a field, as in `(assoc point :x 1 :y 2)`. An unknown\nfield is an error, and a keyword without a value is an error. The call moves\neach field it does not list, unless the field is `copy`.\n\nAny other key updates a vector or a map, as in `(assoc items 0 9)`. Keys and\nvalues come in pairs, and each pair updates the result of the one before.\nThe call moves the collection.\n\nA vector index is an [int], and the call panics when the index is negative\nor out of bounds. A map key has the type of the map's keys: a string literal\nis a `(ref str)`, so a [string] key takes `(str \"name\")`, and a\n`(ref str)` key takes a literal or `(as-str name)`. A map adds a missing key.\n\n(assoc (vec 1 2) 0 9)\n=> (vec 9 2)\n\n(-> (sorted-map \"pear\" 3) (assoc \"fig\" 1) len)\n=> 2")
